\title{A Counting-Theoretic Proof of the Parity Theorem for Eulerian Trails
in Finite Multigraphs with Loops}
\author{The Catalog Project}
\date{2026-06-16}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, self-contained development of the classical parity (degree)
theory of Eulerian trails in finite undirected multigraphs that may contain
loops and parallel edges. The entire theory is reduced to a single local
identity at each vertex,
\[
\deeg(v) + s(v) + e(v) = 2\,\vis(v),
\]
where $\deeg(v)$ is the degree of $v$ (with loops counted twice), $s(v)$ and
$e(v)$ are indicator quantities for $v$ being the start or end of the trail, and
$\vis(v)$ is the number of times the trail's underlying walk visits $v$. The
identity is established by elementary finite counting: by splitting the walk
either at its head or its tail, the visit count is expressed in two complementary
ways, and the degree is matched to consecutive-pair incidences through the edge
permutation that defines the trail. From this identity we deduce, with only
integer parity arithmetic, the three pillars of the classical theory: (i) any
vertex of odd degree must be the start or the end of the trail; (ii) at most two
vertices have odd degree; and (iii) if the trail is closed, every vertex has even
degree. The treatment makes explicit the role of loops, of edge orientation, and
of the bijective (``permutation'') structure of an Eulerian trail.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Eulerian trail, Eulerian circuit, degree, parity,
handshaking, multigraph, loop, graph theory, combinatorics.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{MSC 2020:} 05C45, 05C30, 05C76.

\section{Introduction}

The problem of the Seven Bridges of K\"onigsberg, resolved by Euler in 1736, is
usually cited as the origin of graph theory. Euler's abstraction --- discard the
geometry, retain only adjacency and the count of incident edges at each vertex ---
yields a remarkably clean criterion for the existence of a walk that traverses
every edge exactly once. The \emph{necessary} condition, the subject of this
paper, is a statement purely about \textbf{degree parity}: in a graph admitting an
Eulerian trail, all but at most two vertices have even degree, and if the trail is
closed then \emph{all} vertices have even degree.

A fully rigorous treatment must confront several details that casual expositions
gloss over:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Loops.} A loop contributes two edge-ends to a single vertex; the
    degree theory is correct only if loops are counted with multiplicity two.
  \item \textbf{Parallel edges.} Multigraphs admit several distinct edges with
    identical endpoints, and the trail must use each exactly once; we encode this
    with a permutation of the edge set.
  \item \textbf{Orientation.} In an undirected graph an edge may be traversed in
    either direction, so the trail's compatibility condition must allow both
    orientations and the counting must be insensitive to the choice.
\end{enumerate}

Our contribution isolates the combinatorial core into one \textbf{local parity
identity} and derives every classical consequence by integer arithmetic.

\section{The combinatorial model}

We work over finite index types throughout. Fix natural numbers $n_V$ (vertices)
and $n_E$ (edges); vertices are indexed by $\{0,\dots,n_V-1\}$ and edges by
$\{0,\dots,n_E-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Multigraph]
A \emph{finite undirected multigraph with loops} on $n_V$ vertices and $n_E$ edges
is a pair of endpoint maps $G = (\edone, \edtwo)$ with
$\edone, \edtwo : \{0,\dots,n_E-1\} \to \{0,\dots,n_V-1\}$. An edge $e$ is a
\textbf{loop} if $\edone(e) = \edtwo(e)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Degree]
The \emph{degree} of a vertex $v$ is
\[
\deeg_G(v) = \bigl|\{\,e : \edone(e) = v\,\}\bigr| + \bigl|\{\,e : \edtwo(e) = v\,\}\bigr|.
\]
A loop at $v$ is counted in both terms, contributing $2$ to $\deeg_G(v)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Eulerian trail]
An \emph{Eulerian trail} on $G$ is a triple $T = (\verts, \pi, \mathrm{adj})$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a \textbf{walk} $\verts : \{0,\dots,n_E\} \to \{0,\dots,n_V-1\}$, a
    sequence of $n_E+1$ vertices;
  \item an \textbf{edge ordering} $\pi$, a permutation of $\{0,\dots,n_E-1\}$;
  \item an \textbf{adjacency condition}: for every step $i \in \{0,\dots,n_E-1\}$,
  \begin{align*}
    &\bigl(\edone(\pi(i)) = \verts(i) \wedge \edtwo(\pi(i)) = \verts(i{+}1)\bigr) \\
    \vee\;&\bigl(\edone(\pi(i)) = \verts(i{+}1) \wedge \edtwo(\pi(i)) = \verts(i)\bigr).
  \end{align*}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Start, end, closed]
The \textbf{start} is $\start(T) = \verts(0)$ and the \textbf{end} is
$\last(T) = \verts(n_E)$. The trail is \textbf{closed} if $\start(T) = \last(T)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Counting functionals}

Fix an Eulerian trail $T$ and a vertex $v$. With $\iv{P}$ the Iverson bracket,
define
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vis(v) &= \textstyle\sum_{j=0}^{n_E} \iv{\verts(j) = v}, &
s(v) &= \iv{\verts(0) = v}, &
e(v) &= \iv{\verts(n_E) = v}, \\
c(v) &= \textstyle\sum_{i=0}^{n_E-1} \iv{\verts(i) = v}, &
d(v) &= \textstyle\sum_{i=0}^{n_E-1} \iv{\verts(i{+}1) = v}. & &
\end{aligned}
\]
Here $c(v)$ counts steps whose first endpoint is $v$, and $d(v)$ counts steps
whose second endpoint is $v$.

\section{The local parity identity}

\begin{lemma}[Split at the tail]\label{lem:tail}
$\vis(v) = c(v) + e(v)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Separate the last position $n_E$ from the sum defining $\vis(v)$:
$\sum_{j=0}^{n_E}\iv{\verts(j)=v} = \sum_{j=0}^{n_E-1}\iv{\verts(j)=v} + \iv{\verts(n_E)=v}$,
the two summands being $c(v)$ and $e(v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Split at the head]\label{lem:head}
$\vis(v) = s(v) + d(v)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Separate the first position $0$:
$\sum_{j=0}^{n_E}\iv{\verts(j)=v} = \iv{\verts(0)=v} + \sum_{j=1}^{n_E}\iv{\verts(j)=v}$.
The first summand is $s(v)$; reindexing $j = i+1$ for $i=0,\dots,n_E-1$ turns the
second into $d(v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degree equals incidence count]\label{lem:deg}
$\deeg_G(v) = c(v) + d(v)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $\deeg_G(v) = \sum_e \iv{\edone(e)=v} + \sum_e \iv{\edtwo(e)=v}$. As $\pi$ is
a permutation of the edge set, reindex each sum by $e = \pi(i)$:
\[
\deeg_G(v) = \sum_i \bigl(\iv{\edone(\pi(i))=v} + \iv{\edtwo(\pi(i))=v}\bigr).
\]
By the adjacency condition, in either orientation the unordered endpoint pair of
$\pi(i)$ equals $\{\verts(i), \verts(i{+}1)\}$, so
$\iv{\edone(\pi(i))=v} + \iv{\edtwo(\pi(i))=v} = \iv{\verts(i)=v} + \iv{\verts(i{+}1)=v}$.
Summing over $i$ gives $c(v) + d(v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local parity identity]\label{thm:parity}
For every vertex $v$ of an Eulerian trail $T$,
\[
\boxed{\;\deeg_G(v) + s(v) + e(v) = 2\,\vis(v).\;}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Adding Lemmas~\ref{lem:tail} and \ref{lem:head} gives
$2\,\vis(v) = (c(v)+e(v)) + (s(v)+d(v)) = (c(v)+d(v)) + s(v) + e(v)$. Substitute
$c(v)+d(v) = \deeg_G(v)$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:deg}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:parity} is an exact equation. The right-hand side is manifestly
even, which drives all parity consequences below.
\end{remark}

\section{Classical structure theorems}

\begin{theorem}[Odd degree forces an endpoint]\label{thm:odd}
If $\deeg_G(v)$ is odd, then $v = \start(T)$ or $v = \last(T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}, $\deeg_G(v) + s(v) + e(v)$ is even. If $\deeg_G(v)$ is
odd then $s(v) + e(v)$ is odd, so at least one of $s(v), e(v)$ equals $1$, i.e.
$\verts(0)=v$ or $\verts(n_E)=v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[At most two odd-degree vertices]\label{thm:two}
The set $\{v : \deeg_G(v)\text{ odd}\}$ has at most two elements.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:odd} it is a subset of $\{\start(T), \last(T)\}$, which has at
most two elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed trails have all-even degree]\label{thm:closed}
If $T$ is closed, then $\deeg_G(v)$ is even for every $v$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $\verts(0) = \verts(n_E)$, we have $s(v) = e(v)$, so $s(v)+e(v) \in \{0,2\}$
is even. Then $\deeg_G(v) = 2\,\vis(v) - s(v) - e(v)$ is even by
Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Euler's necessary criterion]
A finite multigraph with loops admits an Eulerian trail only if it has at most two
vertices of odd degree, and a closed Eulerian trail only if it has none. The Seven
Bridges of K\"onigsberg graph, whose four landmasses all have odd degree, admits
no Eulerian trail of any kind.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Theorems~\ref{thm:odd}--\ref{thm:closed} are statements about the degree sequence
of $G$, but their proofs consume a trail $T$ as a witness: existence of $T$ is
exactly what licenses the permutation reindexing in Lemma~\ref{lem:deg} and the
walk decompositions in Lemmas~\ref{lem:tail}--\ref{lem:head}.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithmic consequences}

Computing all degrees and the odd-degree set takes $O(n_E + n_V)$ time by a single
pass that increments two counters per edge (loops automatically count twice). By
Theorem~\ref{thm:two}, more than two odd vertices certifies non-existence. When the
parity test passes and the graph is connected on its non-isolated vertices,
Hierholzer's algorithm constructs an Eulerian trail in $O(n_E)$ time; the parity
identity is the invariant ensuring the greedy walk can only stall at an endpoint.
For graphs with $2k>0$ odd vertices, the Chinese Postman optimum reduces to a
minimum-weight perfect matching on the odd vertices, since
Theorem~\ref{thm:two} localizes the traversal defect to exactly those vertices.

\begin{verbatim}
function EULERIAN_PARITY_AUDIT(endpt1[0..E-1], endpt2[0..E-1], V):
    deg <- array of V zeros
    for e in 0..E-1:
        deg[endpt1[e]] += 1
        deg[endpt2[e]] += 1     # loop hits same index twice
    odd <- { v : deg[v] is odd }
    if |odd| = 0: return ("closed trail possible", odd)
    if |odd| = 2: return ("open trail possible, endpoints = odd", odd)
    return ("no Eulerian trail", odd)
\end{verbatim}

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Genome assembly:} de Bruijn graph reconstruction reduces to Eulerian
trails; the parity criterion governs feasibility. \textbf{Route inspection
(Chinese Postman):} street-sweeping and meter-reading seek least-cost covering
closed walks, with the even-degree characterization as the optimality boundary.
\textbf{Fabrication toolpaths:} plotting and laser cutting minimize travel via
Eulerian traversals. \textbf{Puzzles:} ``draw without lifting the pen'' is the
open-trail problem, decided instantly by the parity test.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\begin{description}
  \item[D1 Sufficiency and connectivity.] Pair the necessary parity certificate
    with a formal Hierholzer correctness proof for a full iff-characterization.
  \item[D2 Global handshake.] Summing the local identity over all vertices yields
    $\sum_v \deeg_G(v) = 2 n_E$, so the number of odd vertices is always even.
  \item[D3 Directed trails.] Replacing the orientation disjunction by a fixed
    orientation yields the in-degree/out-degree theory via signed counts.
  \item[D4 Weighted refinements.] Carrying edge weights through the functionals
    formalizes the Chinese Postman matching reduction.
  \item[D5 Loop calculus.] Tracking loops as a separate term $2\cdot\mathrm{loops}(v)$
    makes self-edge contributions explicit and eases hypergraph generalizations.
\end{description}

\section{Conclusion}

The parity theory of Eulerian trails rests on one exact local identity,
$\deeg(v) + s(v) + e(v) = 2\,\vis(v)$, proved by two walk decompositions and one
permutation reindexing. From it the classical structure theorems follow by integer
parity alone. Nearly three centuries after K\"onigsberg, the necessary criterion
is reduced to its irreducible combinatorial atom.

\end{document}

